<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements for the Snug desktop shell (TASK-20260824, ADR-0047 §7).

  Notarization REQUIRES the hardened runtime, and the hardened runtime blocks the JIT
  that WKWebView's JavaScript engine needs. These two entitlements buy back exactly
  that, and nothing else.

  WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: entitlements govern the SHELL PROCESS, not the app iframe.
  C1 (token boundary) and C2 (sandbox integrity) are enforced by the CSP, the
  `sandbox="allow-scripts"` attribute and the capability allowlist — none of which
  this file can weaken. Adding a broader entitlement here would not widen the app
  sandbox, but it WOULD widen the shell's own attack surface, so the rule is: add
  nothing that a failing launch has not actually demanded.

  Deliberately ABSENT (and why):
    - com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation — would let unsigned dylibs load
      into the shell. The WhatsApp helper is a separate PROCESS, not a library, so it
      does not need this.
    - com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables — an injection vector.
    - com.apple.security.app-sandbox — the App Store sandbox, rejected for this
      distribution channel by ADR-0047 (conflicts with the shell's file/socket surface).
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- WKWebView's JavaScriptCore JIT. Without this the hub renders but every app is
	     unusably slow, and some JS engines refuse to start at all. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- JavaScriptCore also maps W^X pages outside the JIT entitlement's scope; the
	     pair is the documented WKWebView combination. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
